.SH NAME
ktm-cstr \- NUL-terminated u8 * strings: formatting, comparison, copying and conversion.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import cstr;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
NUL-terminated u8 * strings: formatting, comparison, copying and conversion.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS cmpLen
.EX
i64 cmpLen(u8 *s1, u8 *s2, i64 len);
.EE
.PP
Compare at most len bytes like C's strncmp: < 0, 0 or > 0, bytes compared as unsigned, stopping at a NUL. null sorts before every string (two NULLs are equal), whatever len is.
.SS cmpLenNoCase
.EX
i64 cmpLenNoCase(u8 *s1, u8 *s2, i64 len);
.EE
.PP
cstr.cmpLen ignoring ASCII case, like C's strncasecmp.
.SS copy
.EX
void copy(u8 *dst, u8 *src);
.EE
.PP
Copy src and its NUL into dst, which must be big enough. A null src copies "" and a null dst does nothing.
.SS cmp
.EX
i64 cmp(u8 *s1, u8 *s2);
.EE
.PP
Compare like C's strcmp: < 0, 0 or > 0, bytes compared as unsigned. null sorts before every string; two NULLs are equal.
.SS toLower
.EX
u8 toLower(u8 ch);
.EE
.PP
ch in lower case: only 'A'-'Z' change (ASCII).
.PP
Only when: IS_X86_64
.SS toUpper
.EX
u8 toUpper(u8 ch);
.EE
.PP
ch in upper case: only 'a'-'z' change (ASCII).
.PP
Only when: IS_X86_64
.SS isSpace
.EX
bool isSpace(u8 ch);
.EE
.PP
true for ' ', '\et', '\en', '\ev', '\ef' and '\er'.
.PP
Only when: IS_X86_64
.SS hash
.EX
u64 hash(u8 *str);
.EE
.PP
A 64-bit hash of str's bytes: h = h * 31 + c from 0, then a final mix (murmur3's), so similar keys spread over the low bits; "" hashes to 0. The same value on every target.
.PP
Only when: IS_X86_64
.SS toInt
.EX
i64 toInt(u8 *str);
.EE
.PP
The integer at the start of str (like atol); 0 for null
.SS view
.EX
u8[] view(u8 *s);
.EE
.PP
The bytes of a C string, before its NUL, as a u8[] (a view, nothing copied; empty for null): where a u8[] is wanted, \fBcstr.view(path)\fR
.SS len
.EX
u64 len(u8 *buf);
.EE
.PP
The length of \fBbuf\fR before its '\e0', 0 for null. libc's strlen, which reads a word at a time: about 5x the old byte loop.
.SS dupLen
.EX
u8 *dupLen(u8 *buf, i64 len);
.EE
.PP
A new string with at most the first \fBlen\fR bytes of buf, stopping at its NUL (like C's strndup); always NUL terminated. A negative len is 0 and a null buf gives "".
.SS dup
.EX
u8 *dup(u8 *buf);
.EE
.PP
A new copy of buf, "" for null; mem.free it
.SS count
.EX
i64 count(u8 *src, i64 ch);
.EE
.PP
How many times the byte ch is in src (0 for null)
.SS firstOf
.EX
u8 *firstOf(u8 *src,u8 *marker);
.EE
.PP
The first byte of src that is one of the bytes in marker, null when none is (or either is null)
.SS lastOf
.EX
u8 *lastOf(u8 *src,u8 *marker);
.EE
.PP
The last byte of src that is one of the bytes in marker, null when none is (or either is null)
.SS quote
.EX
u8 *quote(u8 *ptr,i64 flags = 0);
.EE
.PP
A new string of ptr escaped for a C literal: \en \er \et \ev \ef \e" \e\e and \exNN for other control bytes (what %Q prints); with PRTF_SLASH each % is doubled too. mem.free it.
.SS formatArgs
.EX
u8 *formatArgs(u8 *_dst, u8 *fmt, u64[] args);
.EE
.PP
formatTo with the arguments in a slice, for functions that take \fB\&...\fR and pass it on
.SS concatArgs
.EX
u8 *concatArgs(u64[] args);
.EE
.PP
concat with the strings in a slice
.SS concat
.EX
u8 *concat(...args);
.EE
.PP
A new string of every argument (C strings) one after another; mem.free it
.SS formatTo
.EX
u8 *formatTo(u8 *dst=null,u8 *fmt,...args);
.EE
.PP
The formatted text written into dst, with no bound (see formatToSize for a fixed buffer); with dst null, into a new block that you mem.free. Returns where it wrote.
.SS formatToSize
.EX
u8 *formatToSize(u8 *dst, i64 size, u8 *fmt, ...args);
.EE
.PP
Format into \fBdst\fR, which has room for \fBsize\fR bytes, like C's snprintf: at most size-1 characters are written, always followed by a '\e0' (size <= 0 writes nothing), and longer output is cut short. Returns dst. cstr.formatTo(dst, ...) has no bound, so use this for a fixed buffer.
.SS format
.EX
u8 *format(u8 *fmt,...args);
.EE
.PP
The formatted text in a new block of exactly its size; mem.free it
.SS appendFormat
.EX
u8 *appendFormat(u8 *_dst,u8 *fmt,...args);
.EE
.PP
Append formatted text to the heap string \fB_dst\fR (mem.alloc, cstr.dup, cstr.formatTo(null, ...) or a previous cstr.appendFormat; null starts a new one), growing it when it is full, and return it. The block may move and the old pointer is then freed, so always write \fBs = cstr.appendFormat(s, ...)\fR and never pass a string literal or a stack buffer. Each call measures the whole of \fB_dst\fR to find its end, so n appends cost O(n^2) for a long string: in a loop keep the length yourself with cstr.appendFormatLen, or collect the pieces and cstr.formatTo them once.
.SS appendFormatLen
.EX
u8 *appendFormatLen(u8 *_dst, i64 *_len, u8 *fmt, ...args);
.EE
.PP
cstr.appendFormat for a string whose length the caller keeps in \fB*_len\fR, so no cstr.len per call. \fB_dst\fR may be null (with *_len 0) to start one.
.SS globMatch
.EX
bool globMatch(u8 *testStr, u8 *wildStr);
.EE
.PP
Wildcard match of the whole of testStr: '*' matches any run of characters (also none) and '?' any one character; everything else matches itself. false if either string is null.
